course: dictionary
lesson: 715
title: Barium, the green metal
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Barium is a chemical element. It is a soft, silvery metal, and its chemical symbol is Ba. Every element has an atomic number, and barium's is 56.
explain: Barium is in the periodic table, the chart of all the elements. «The colours of fireworks come from different metals: strontium gives red, barium green, sodium yellow and copper blue.» So barium is the metal that makes fireworks glow green.
explain: Barium has another meaning too. In a hospital, a barium meal is a white drink with barium in it. The patient swallows it, and it shows up on an X-ray, so doctors can see the stomach clearly. This drink is only given under a doctor's care.
fact: Barium is a chemical element, which means it cannot be broken down into simpler substances.
fact: Barium is a soft, silvery metal with the symbol Ba and atomic number 56.
fact: Fireworks use barium to make a green colour.
fact: A barium meal is a white drink that patients swallow so their stomach shows up on an X-ray.
fact: Only doctors give a barium meal. Never try to swallow barium metal or any other chemical from a lab.
exercise: «The colours of fireworks come from different metals: strontium gives red, barium green, sodium yellow and copper blue.» Which colour does barium give? | *Green | Red | Blue | Yellow
exercise: What kind of substance is barium? | A type of X-ray machine | A liquid you can pour | A coloured firework | *A soft, silvery metal
exercise: In a hospital, what is a barium meal? | A firework that makes green sparks | A big dinner for patients | *A white drink that shows the stomach on an X-ray | A metal spoon for eating
test: «Every kind of metal gives back its own colour: strontium red, barium green, sodium yellow, copper blue.» Which metal gives blue? | Strontium | Sodium | *Copper | Barium
test: What does the symbol Ba stand for? | Boron | Bromine | *Barium | Bismuth
exam: What do doctors use a barium meal for? | To make a metal shine | *To see the stomach clearly on an X-ray | To cool down a hot metal | To make fireworks green
